EXPERIMENT: IMAGE GALLERY USING HTML AND DOCKER

AIM

1. Build an HTML page with a gallery of 3–4 images with captions.
2. Create a Docker image for the HTML gallery application and run it using port mapping.

SOFTWARE REQUIRED

- Notepad
- Docker Desktop
- Command Prompt
- Google Chrome

FILES REQUIRED

Create a folder named "Gallery" on Desktop.

Create two files inside it:

1. index.html
2. Dockerfile

PROGRAM 1: index.html

<html>
<head>
    <title>My Gallery</title>
</head>
<body style="text-align:center; background-color:lightblue;">

    <h1>My Image Gallery</h1>

    <img src="https://picsum.photos/id/10/200/150">
    <h3>Nature</h3>

    <img src="https://picsum.photos/id/20/200/150">
    <h3>Forest</h3>

    <img src="https://picsum.photos/id/30/200/150">
    <h3>Flowers</h3>

    <img src="https://picsum.photos/id/40/200/150">
    <h3>Mountains</h3>

</body>
</html>

Save as "index.html" inside Gallery.

EXECUTION OF EXPERIMENT 1

Double-click "index.html". It opens in Chrome and displays four images with captions.

PROGRAM 2: Dockerfile

FROM nginx:alpine

COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/

EXPOSE 80

Save as "Dockerfile" inside the same Gallery folder.

EXECUTION OF EXPERIMENT 2 USING CMD

First, open Docker Desktop and wait until the engine is running.

Open CMD and execute these commands one by one.

Step 1: Open the Gallery folder

cd /d "%USERPROFILE%\Desktop\Gallery"

Step 2: Build Docker image

docker build -t mygallery .

Step 3: Check Docker image

docker images

Step 4: Run Docker container with automatic port mapping

docker run -d -p 80 --name gallery mygallery

Step 5: Check running container

docker ps




Step 6: Check the PORTS column.

Example: "0.0.0.0:49153->80/tcp"

Step 7: Open Chrome and enter:

"http://localhost:49153"

Replace 49153 with the actual port displayed in CMD.







IMPORTANT DOCKER COMMANDS

docker build -t mygallery .
docker images
docker run -d -p 80 --name gallery mygallery
docker ps
docker stop gallery
docker rm -f gallery

RESULT

Successfully created an HTML image gallery containing four images with captions, built a Docker image, and executed the gallery application inside a Docker container using automatic port mapping.



